{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA (rebar-debug) — proven root cause + attribution. STOPPED at exit gate; remediation is a DESIGN DECISION escalated to the operator.\n\nInvestigated on a fresh worktree off origin/main @ 55589c15 (= reported-against commit). NO live Bedrock/gate call was made — the classification path was reproduced entirely locally with an injected/mocked provider. (The claim of THIS bug succeeded with no Bedrock load: a bug is not plan-review claim-gated.)\n\n### Proven mechanism (H1 wins) — throttle → failover → keyless fallback → INDETERMINATE\n\nUnder concurrent multi-session load the SHARED Bedrock account (896586841071/us-east-1) throttles at the ACCOUNT level (TPM/RPM). The frontier chain is `frontier = { model = \"bedrock:opus\", fallback = [{ model = \"anthropic:opus\" }] }` (rebar.toml). The failure taxonomy classifies a plain rate-limit 429 (ThrottlingException) as `WAIT_AND_RETRY` (retryable), and `model_classes.should_fall_back` fails over the chain for every retryable/`CHANGE_PROVIDER_OR_MODEL` class — so a throttle triggers failover to `anthropic:opus`. In these environments `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is ABSENT, so the anthropic provider raises `TypeError: Could not resolve authentication method…`. `should_fall_back(TypeError)` is False (NEEDS_INVESTIGATION), so pydantic-ai's `FallbackModel` re-raises the bare `TypeError`; `failure.classify_llm_failure` maps an unmatched `TypeError` → `NEEDS_INVESTIGATION` (non-retryable) → the gate emits the hard **INDETERMINATE** verdict (`_cli/_llm_commands._disposition_exit_code`), NOT the retryable exit-11. Because the account is shared, ALL concurrent sessions throttle and fail over at once → **fleet-wide simultaneous INDETERMINATE**.\n\nThe crux: a throttle that SHOULD be a benign retryable exit-11 (\"back off and retry\") is CONVERTED into a hard INDETERMINATE by the failover to a keyless fallback whose terminal auth error masks the retryable primary cause.\n\n### Runtime proof (dynamic hypothesis, proven by execution — not source-reading)\n\nInjected/mocked provider, no real Bedrock call. Full reproducer script content preserved below.\n\n- **Exp A — plain Bedrock throttle (429 rate_limit) ALONE:** `should_fall_back=True`; verdict = **EXIT_11_RETRYABLE** (`WAIT_AND_RETRY`). A throttle by itself is retryable/benign.\n- **Exp B — the keyless-anthropic auth `TypeError` (the EXACT string recorded in session log 4f6b / inner-amiable-grebe):** `should_fall_back=False`; verdict = **INDETERMINATE** (`NEEDS_INVESTIGATION`, `exception_type=TypeError` — matches the recorded diagnostic byte-for-byte).\n- **Exp C — DECISIVE, real pydantic-ai `FallbackModel` + the real `should_fall_back` predicate:** primary raises throttle(429), fallback raises the keyless auth `TypeError`. Observed: primary attempted 1×, **failover FIRED** (fallback attempted 1×), the `TypeError` escaped the chain, final verdict = **INDETERMINATE**. This discriminates H1 (failover-to-keyless-fallback) from \"throttle alone\" (Exp A = exit 11): the failover is what converts a retryable throttle into a hard INDETERMINATE.\n\nCorroborating live evidence: session log **4f6b (inner-amiable-grebe)** records the exact incident and the operator's workaround verbatim: \"local LLM gates (review-plan/review-code/verify-completion) fail with 'Could not resolve authentication method' when Bedrock opus throttles — the frontier class falls over to a keyless anthropic fallback (no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY here). FIX: run gates with REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE=$PWD/.github/llm-providers/bedrock.toml (bedrock-only, no fallback) so throttled calls retry on Bedrock.\" Plus \"6 rapid reviews likely exhausted TPM quota.\"\n\n### H2 (stale-store gate refusal) — considered and REJECTED for THIS symptom\n\nCommit 2598909d (\"Refuse gate certification on a stale store\") adds freshness checks to the CLAIM gate, the plan-review CLOSE gate, and the completion CLOSE gate, each emitting the DISTINCT verdict `freshness.STALE_VERDICT == 'stale-store'` (verified at runtime) \"before any attestation is consulted\". That is a different observable from the reported \"review-plan returns INDETERMINATE\", and it is NOT on the `review-plan` LLM verdict path (`plan_review.review-plan --status` is read-only, no LLM). Stale-store can co-occur at CLAIM time under the same concurrent-write storm, but it does not explain the reported INDETERMINATE. H1 alone explains every reported symptom (fleet-wide simultaneity via the shared account; the exact `TypeError` diagnostic; \"unsigned, no determinate verdict\").\n\n### Defect legitimacy = ESCALATE (design decision; NOT auto-fixable against an unambiguous contract)\n\nThree layers, only the third is arguably a code defect:\n1. **Operational trigger** — self-inflicted account throttle from concurrent frontier/opus fan-out (~70 worktrees). No single code defect; a load-management concern.\n2. **Environment** — `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` absent, contrary to the config's documented expectation (\"In normal local/CI operation, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is EXPECTED to be present and VALID\"). environment-or-config.\n3. **Latent disposition-masking defect** — even granting (1)+(2), the surfaced disposition is arguably WRONG: a retryable throttle is presented as a non-retryable INDETERMINATE because the chain-failure classification keeps only the TERMINAL (fallback) exception and discards the retryable PRIMARY cause. This defeats the retryable-taxonomy INTENT (`WAIT_AND_RETRY` → \"wait for the backoff window, then retry\" → exit 11). Whether to change this is a DESIGN DECISION.\n\nPer the /rebar-debug not-a-bug + design-decision guard, I STOPPED at the Phase 1 exit gate and did NOT auto-apply a fix.\n\n### Candidate remediation directions (operator's call — tradeoffs)\n\n- **(A) Operational / concurrency** — add a client-side concurrency limiter / rate-limit on frontier `review-plan` so the shared account isn't overrun (defense in depth; also cuts token spend). In-process, portable. Does not by itself fix the disposition-masking.\n- **(B) Config/env** — either ensure `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is present in agent environments (failover then succeeds transparently), OR adopt the operator's 4f6b workaround (`REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE=…/bedrock.toml`, no fallback) so a throttle stays exit-11 retryable. Zero code change; per-environment.\n- **(C) Code / disposition (RECOMMENDED, see report)** — stop a throttled primary from being downgraded to INDETERMINATE by a non-retryable fallback failure. Two sub-options: (c1) exclude plain `WAIT_AND_RETRY` (rate-limit) from `should_fall_back` so a throttle RETRIES the primary instead of failing over — symmetric with the existing \"a credential error must NOT fail over\" rule and matching AWS adaptive-retry / OSS best practice for 429s; or (c2) when a `FallbackModel` chain exhausts, classify by the MOST-retryable member so a throttle+authfail group surfaces as `WAIT_AND_RETRY` (exit 11). (c1) is simpler and directly matches the operator's own conclusion.\n\n### Attribution (candidate — NOT linked pending the defect-vs-config decision)\n\nThe disposition-masking became REACHABLE when the anthropic fallback was added to the frontier chain: **`8fbd-e9d5-326e-40d8`** (commit `6d4f394195b2` \"Enable class-scoped Anthropic fallback\", 2026-08-10). Before it, frontier had NO fallback, so a throttle → `WAIT_AND_RETRY` → exit-11 retryable (no INDETERMINATE). Substrate: the failover machinery `should_fall_back` (cc33-13db-de48-4468, commit 72e59d982fee, 2026-08-02) and the closed resolution-disposition classifier (civilized-immediate-mamba, commit 4291b9a5600e, 2026-07-10). 8fbd was a DELIBERATE operator-approved decision (\"Direct Anthropic is the approved fallback for frontier and standard\"), so this is not a regression-defect; `caused_by` is intentionally NOT linked yet because whether this is a `plan_defect` vs `env_integration` disposition is part of what is being escalated. Recommended `--caused-by 8fbd-e9d5-326e-40d8` IF the operator elects the code fix (C).\n\n### Relationship to 35ab-f0e0 (fightable-nirvanic-kitten, in_progress, another agent)\n\n35ab is a SIBLING at the same classification seam but a DIFFERENT trigger: a bare `WorkflowError` (unknown scripted `uses:` step) mis-mapping to `llm_unavailable`. This incident's trigger is a provider throttle→failover→keyless-auth `TypeError`→`NEEDS_INVESTIGATION`. They share the downstream property that a non-outage condition collapses to a non-retryable verdict, but they are distinct mechanisms with distinct fixes; not duplicates. Coordinated via a note on 35ab.\n\n### Reproducer (DEBUG-rebar-debug; no real provider call)\n\n```python\nfrom rebar.llm.failure import classify_llm_failure, ClassifyContext\nfrom rebar.llm.model_classes import should_fall_back\n\ndef verdict_for(exc):\n    o = classify_llm_failure(exc, ClassifyContext(model=\"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\"))\n    return (\"EXIT_11_RETRYABLE\" if o.retryable else \"INDETERMINATE\", o.resolution_class.value,\n            o.diagnostic.get(\"exception_type\"))\n\nfrom pydantic_ai.exceptions import ModelHTTPError\nthrottle = ModelHTTPError(status_code=429, model_name=\"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\",\n    body={\"error\": {\"type\": \"rate_limit_error\", \"message\": \"ThrottlingException\"}})\nauth = TypeError(\"Could not resolve authentication method. Expected one of api_key, auth_token, or credentials to be set. …\")\n\n# A: should_fall_back(throttle)=True ; verdict=('EXIT_11_RETRYABLE','WAIT_AND_RETRY','ModelHTTPError')\n# B: should_fall_back(auth)=False   ; verdict=('INDETERMINATE','NEEDS_INVESTIGATION','TypeError')\n# C: real FallbackModel(primary=throttle, fallback=auth, fallback_on=should_fall_back), Agent.run(...)\n#    -> failover fires, TypeError escapes, verdict=('INDETERMINATE','NEEDS_INVESTIGATION','TypeError')\n```\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788046367054894686,"uuid":"f3259a52-0a94-404a-b018-524187294b3b"}